  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that $x=5$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+29x-45=0,$ find the remaining solutions.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+29x-45.$ Since $x=5$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{5} & 1 & -9 & 29 & -45 \\\\  &  & 5 & -20 & 45 \\\\  & 1 & -4 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-5)({x}^{2}-4x+9).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-4x+9=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-5 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=5,\\,2\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$",
